ushing  machines in in corner shop yes is that  the way you are envisaging the one in  Britain going that's one of the ways in  which you could go and that's one of the  reasons why we're having a consultation  in order to make sure that we listen to  small business those those corners shop  stores that that might be responsible  for having some of these machinery and  but also that we want to talk more  broadly to local government as well  because they're responsible at the  moment for so much recycling we want to  make sure that every single part of the  chain can work effectively in order to  ensure the silver  economic considerations on this local  authorities might lose out on the  revenue from recycling and indeed the  corner shops might lose out there's a  big lot of floor space going for these  machines one imagines well it's it is  the case in other countries that people  often want to have these machines  because they draw in traffic actually  and they ensure that there is more  fruitful but it is the case when we look  at local government if it's the case  that we're going to have plastic bottles  going into these machines rather than  going into our bins and going into local  authority recycling we need to ensure  that local authorities get the support  that they need that's why we're also  looking at changing the way in which  producers and all those who use plastic  actually pay into a system which helps  local government and help central  government to recycle more effective  where they pay for the machines as well  well one of the things we need to do is  to be in a position to though whether  it's the soft drink manufacturers or  whether it's others business has to step  up business has to make a contribution  they're the people who are using this  plastic and we need to have a scheme  that doesn't just raise the revenue for  recycling but also creates an incentive  for those manufacturers to move away  sometimes from using what's called  virgin material ie plastic that hasn't  itself been recycled towards more  recycled material it's trying to make  sure that you get a virtuous circle are  you using data from the plastic bag  scheme which has been a tremendous  success in in cutting though in cutting  down that usage and therefore thinking  about the the pricing here what yes AWS  it might be put on the bottles  absolutely there's a there's the you're  absolutely right that the plastic bag  scheme has been a huge success and as a  result we've seen a more than eighty  percent reduction in plastic bag use as  a result of a simple challenge being  placed on all bags if we look at other  countries whether that's Germany or  Sweden that have used deposit return  schemes they have a variable rate again  one of the things we want to consult on  is what's the right rate in order to  encourage the right level of recycling  and at the same time to make it  effective and efficient for industry  whether those are the soft drink  companies or the the corner shops that  might be installing some of those  deposit machines and just lastly can I  push you for a date when you think it  might come into operation subject to  consultation we've got all that what  about a year's time to coincide with  brexit I think it would be a great  brexit bonus but I didn't want it to be  specifically told I'm sorry to be  open-minded on this issue I need to make  sure that the consultation listens to  everyone because we've got to get it  right it's better to get it right and to  ensure that by the time certainly well  before the next election that people see  this scheme up and running they're  confident that we've done the right  thing
 

